course: dictionary
lesson: 359
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the context, the words around it. «Insects smell and feel with their antennae.» Insects are animals, so here an antenna must be a feeler on an insect's head.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «A phone mast has antennas that send and receive radio signals.» Phones and signals tell you this antenna is a metal rod or wire, not a feeler.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. Does «The card has a tiny antenna coil inside» still make sense if you think of feelers? No, so it must mean the radio kind.
fact: Antenna has two main meanings: a feeler on an insect's head, and a metal rod or wire that sends and receives radio, TV or phone signals.
fact: For insects, the plural is antennae, as in «a pair of antennae». For radio, the plural is usually antennas.
fact: Insects use their antennae to feel and smell. Water fleas jump through water with theirs.
fact: Spiders have eight legs and no antennae.
fact: Never climb a phone mast or its fence.
exercise: «Insects smell and feel with their antennae.» What does antennae mean here? | A phone mast | *Feelers on an insect's head | A bus card reader | A metal rod that sends radio signals
exercise: «A phone mast has antennas that send and receive radio signals.» What are the antennas for? | Catching fish | Climbing up high | *Sending and receiving radio signals | Feeling and smelling
exercise: Which word is the plural of antenna for an insect? | antenna's | *antennae | antennas | antenna
test: «Spiders have eight legs and two body parts, and no antennae.» Why does the sentence say no antennae? | Antennae means legs | *Spiders do not have feelers on their heads | Spiders are not alive | Spiders have too many legs to have antennae
test: Dad says the TV antenna is broken. What is the antenna here? | A kind of bus stop | *A metal rod that catches TV signals | A fishing net | A feeler on a bug
exam: «Tapping a card on a bus reader pays the fare by a short radio signal; the card has a tiny antenna coil inside.» What does antenna mean here? | *A tiny coil that sends and receives radio signals | A feeler on an insect's head | A seat on the bus | A kind of soft toy
